Subject: [PATCH] Lichii #1056 (with #1057 and #1055): 3D macros that read memory run on the split's back; command list fetches wait only for the last writer before the last host sync (EDEN_UP_L1056_BACK_MACROS, EDEN_UP_L1056_LIST_FETCH, EDEN_UP_L1055_LIST_FETCH, EDEN_UP_L1056_STATS; all off by default)

Port of zortos293/Lichii #1056 (zortos293's port of jpkto's #805 back macros, merged into nx2 as
1a5e995a38 on 9 Nov: aa3853e6ea and 26aad9dc55 = jpkto's 6229487c8c/cb4c21d8ca and
a442c38fe6/05a14638a1 from #805, 5bd45fb95c and 8d42747683 by zortos293; capy-ai co-authored the
last three). Compared with #1057 (jpkto, closed for #1056) and #1055 (robertveloso, closed for
#1056). #1032 (estevanbs) is not ported (see the end).

#1056 or #1057: the same two #805 commits with the same code where it matters (the analysis in
macro_turing.h, macro.h, the back macro code in maxwell_3d.cpp; checked hunk by hunk). #1057 keeps
EDEN_NX2_BACK_MACROS=0 and [[unlikely]]; #1056 drops both and adds 5bd45fb95c (counters) and
8d42747683 (review fix: the list wait targets the last writer sent before the last sync instead of
min(last writer, last sync), and the page bits are read again after the wait whether or not it
waited). Taken: #1056 (a superset); #1057's off switch is the EDEN_UP switch here.

Commit by commit (have = in T84 already; line numbers are T84's):
- aa3853e6ea list waits up to the last host sync (jpkto's 6229487c8c): have, as
  EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_LISTSYNC (0474, Fritchii 6baafdd7d9 carries the same commit):
  GpuSplit::WaitForSyncedWriters (gpu_split.cpp:496) waits for min(last writer, last host-level
  wait); NoteListSync (gpu_split.h:410) notes GP entry sync bits (dma_pusher.cpp:675), 3D and
  puller waits for idle (NoteSync, gpu_split.h:383), syncpoint acquires and every semaphore
  acquire, however met, and every resumed acquire (puller.cpp:551, 621, 712, 853).
- 26aad9dc55 back macros (jpkto's 05a14638a1): missing. T84's front runs every 3D macro itself
  (FrontRunMacro, maxwell_3d.cpp:1834); its MME DMA reads (FrontDmaRead, :1999) drain the back, or
  with EDEN_NX2_DMA_SNAPSHOT=1 (play env) skip the drain for pages a drained read flushed whole that
  nothing sent since may write (:2034-2140; misses drain: the "dma snapshot miss" site).
  DMA_READ_AHEAD (:1895), SEM_FAST and DRAIN_SKIP_SAME narrow other drains; nothing moves a macro
  off the front. TuringMacroInterpreter (macro_turing.h) is byte for byte upstream's base, so
  AnalyzeEffects applies as is. Ported: EDEN_UP_L1056_BACK_MACROS.
- 5bd45fb95c counters (zortos293): missing (they extend #1028's async read-back line, which T84
  does not have). Ported as EDEN_UP_L1056_STATS and two drain sites.
- 8d42747683 review fix (zortos293): partly. The second look after a pass that found nothing queued
  exists as EDEN_NX2_FETCH_RECHECK=1 (dma_pusher.cpp:417, :767; not in the play env, so LISTSYNC
  runs without it); the narrower target is missing. Ported: EDEN_UP_L1056_LIST_FETCH (both).
- Tests (turing_macro_effects.cpp, the split test in maxwell_fast_method.cpp): not ported (our
  tests are not built); turing_macro_effects.cpp runs standalone in the checks.

#1055, LISTSYNC and #1056, precisely. The wait before a command entry not on known GPU-written
pages goes to a ring position; LW = after the last compute/copy writer sent, LS = the last
host-level wait noted, LW@LS = LW as it was at LS:
- T84 unset: LW (every queued writer; draws never).
- LISTSYNC (0474; = #805's 6229487c8c = #1056's first commit before its review): min(LW, LS).
- #1056 as merged (EDEN_UP_L1056_LIST_FETCH): LW@LS, which is <= min(LW, LS). The same as LISTSYNC
  while no writer was sent since the last sync; narrower once one was (LISTSYNC then waits for
  everything up to the sync, #1056 only up to the last writer before it). Plus the second look.
- #1055 (EDEN_UP_L1055_LIST_FETCH): LS, which is >= min(LW, LS). Never narrower than LISTSYNC, and
  wider than T84 whenever the last sync came after the last writer (it then also waits for the
  draws and other work between them). Its one thing the others lack: it orders draws before the
  last sync too (a page a draw wrote first; T84 never waited for draws). Same second look.
So #1055 is not LISTSYNC: it is stricter (waits more), not a narrowing of T84, and complementary
only in covering draw writers; its author closed it for #1056. Its EDEN_NX2_PERF_VERIFY (wait for
every writer as well, log entries a later writer wrote) is not ported: LISTSYNC's back-side check
(CheckListWriters, no wait) logs the same entries and now runs for both rules too. All three rules
take the same sync notes (ours note every acquire however met, not only drained ones as upstream:
more syncs only mean more waiting). LISTSKIP's skip still applies when set.

What each switch does (Common::PeSwitch, exactly "1"; read once):
EDEN_UP_L1056_BACK_MACROS=1 (needs the front macros: EDEN_NX2_SPLIT=2, the default, with the macro
  JIT; else inert): at each 3D macro call the front looks the macro up once per macro code
  generation. TuringMacroInterpreter::AnalyzeEffects follows the code from the call's start along
  every path (each branch either way, each loop any number of times) with the register, carry and
  method values the code fixes, and lists the methods it can write and whether it uses the data RAM.
  A macro that writes MME_DMA_READ_FIFOED (0x158), uses no data RAM and writes only back-writable
  registers goes to the back as one ring entry with its parameters (GpuSplit::DeferWords). The back
  runs it as ProcessMacro would (ConsumeSink, then MacroEngine::Execute) with a MacroEngine of its
  own (a copy of the code, its own JIT program, its own data RAM): its DMA reads flush and read at
  its point of the stream, as a drained front read did, and the front neither drains nor looks up
  a DMA snapshot for them. Registers such a macro can write are the back's until the front writes
  them (FrontTakeOver: the write is sent even if the front's copy holds the value) or reads them
  (FrontRegister, from a front macro's register read or FrontDmaRead's 0x154-0x156: waits until the
  back ran that macro, site "back register", and takes the back's value); in a tracked shadow RAM
  mode their shadow entries too (a replay takes the entry from the back first). A macro code change
  waits for the queued back macros (site "back code") and analyses again. A back macro that writes
  outside its predicted set (counted on the back) runs on the front from then on, after a drain and
  a resync of every front register (FrontVerify). Back-writable: non-executable 3D registers below
  0xe00, the root table methods, render_enable.mode, clear_report_value and the fragment, texture
  and tiled cache barriers; never const_buffer.*, report_semaphore.*, upload.* to launch_dma,
  load_mme.start_address_ptr, shadow_scratch, logic_op.enable or 0x157, and (not upstream) never
  wait_for_idle (our front notes it: DMA read-ahead and list syncs) nor, with EDEN_UP_UBO_SNAPSHOT=1,
  the bind groups (FrontNoteBind). In Ocarina the render enable macro qualifies (upstream: code at
  0x142, writes 0x154, 0x155, 0x158 and 0x556; it is the source of the ~7,000 macro DMA reads a
  second of the T84 walk, by the DMA snapshot line of t84-b run642: 262,144 reads in 37.5 s; the
  8 Nov drain map counted ~11,800/s). The first 32
  analysed macros that read memory are logged ("EDEN_UP_L1056_BACK_MACROS: split macro at ...
  reads memory and runs on the back: writes ..." or "... on the front: <reason>").
EDEN_UP_L1056_LIST_FETCH=1: the command entry wait goes to LW@LS (GpuSplit::WaitForLastSyncedWriter;
  NoteListSync now also stores synced_writer_position), and when it found nothing queued the page
  bits are read again (a writer the back ran since the first look may have marked them). With
  LISTSYNC set as well, this target replaces LISTSYNC's (LISTSKIP's skip stays). Entries that
  skipped a writer are checked on the back as LISTSYNC's are ("EDEN_UP_L1056_LIST_FETCH: command
  entry at ... was GPU-written by work queued before it and read from its submit copy"; must stay 0).
EDEN_UP_L1055_LIST_FETCH=1: the target is LS (GpuSplit::WaitForListSync), with the same second look
  and check. EDEN_UP_L1056_LIST_FETCH wins when both are set. Ported for completeness and as a
  stricter fallback; see Expected before giving it a slot.
Diagnostics: EDEN_UP_L1056_STATS=1 logs every 10 s "Lichii #1056 in 10 s (EDEN_UP_L1056_BACK_MACROS=,
  list fetch rule, LISTSYNC/LISTSKIP mode): back macros: S calls sent to the back (B while it still
  had work queued: front waits avoided), R run there (X ms), their DMA reads N (F flushed
  GPU-modified words), K macro DMA reads left on the front; front waits for back macros: ... back
  register (ms), ... back code (ms), T registers taken over, E escapes; list entries not known
  GPU-written L: would wait by T84's rule a, LISTSYNC's b, #1056's c, #1055's d; waited w (the
  active rule), r found GPU-written by the second look". Each rule's verdict is taken before the
  wait, on the same entries, so the control's line says how many of T84's list waits each rule
  would drop. Its only effect besides counting: NoteListSync records its two positions, which only
  a list rule reads. Give it to the control too. EDEN_NX2_SPLIT_WAITS gets the sites "back register"
  and "back code".

Deviations from upstream: everything behind the switches (upstream always on), and the list rule
and the back macros as separate switches (upstream: one PR); wait_for_idle and, with UBO_SNAPSHOT,
the bind groups are not back-writable; counters are EDEN_UP_L1056_STATS, not #1028's async
read-back line (no #1028 here); the sync notes are LISTSYNC's (every acquire however met); the back
macro's DMA read takes T84's MacroEngine::Execute path (QueryDma BeforeRead, FlushRegion of the
bytes read when dirty, AfterBufferFlush) instead of upstream's QueryDma::FlushPending, the same
path T84's drained front read defers; DeferWords has no Writes argument (no #1028 write tracking;
a back macro writes no memory: report, upload, inline data and launch_dma methods are not
back-writable); no ReleaseFront change (our front state lives as long as the engine); #1055's
"list sync" site is not added (its waits count under "list writers", so arms compare directly).
#1057's EDEN_NX2_BACK_MACROS=0 and #805's TEST commit (EDEN_NX2_BACK_MACRO_VERIFY, left out upstream
too) are not ported; EDEN_NX2_SPLIT_VERIFY=1 is the verify run here.

Unset (all switches): T84 behaviour. What still runs: one static switch test per front 3D macro
call (FrontRunOnBack returns at once), a bitset test per front register write, multi-method write,
macro register read and FrontDmaRead register read, a null test per macro write and DMA read in
MacroEngine::Execute, CountListRules' early return and one more false test on list entries that
did not wait, a switch test in NoteListSync, the report guards in MaybeStart; new members
(FrontState: two u64 arrays of 0xe00 entries and a bitset, ~57 KB per 3D engine's front state;
MacroEngine: a pointer and a counter; GpuSplit: three words), two more drain sites (array sizes).
CheckListWriters builds its message from strings now; the LISTSYNC text is unchanged.

Risks:
- BACK_MACROS: the front reading a register a back macro wrote through a path that bypasses
  FrontRegister/FrontTakeOver would use a stale copy. Audited: every fs.regs read in maxwell_3d.cpp
  (report_semaphore, const_buffer, upload, load_mme, shadow_scratch and shadow_ram_control are not
  back-writable; 0x154-0x156 and macro register reads go through FrontRegister; every 3D write the
  front sends goes through FrontWrite or FrontCallMultiMethod). EDEN_NX2_SPLIT_VERIFY=1 would log
  "Split front register ..." differences if one slipped.
- The render enable decision now reads its report pair at the back's point of the stream instead
  of a DMA snapshot hit; that is the exact read (the drained one), so a difference from T84 would
  mean a snapshot hit was stale. Watch flashes and pop-in all the same.
- AnalyzeEffects wrong for some real macro: the back counts the stray write, the macro goes back to
  the front with one drain and a full register resync (an "escapes" count above 0 says so).
- Back cost: ~7,000 calls a second in the walk run on the back with their dirty checks, flushes and
  query hooks (upstream PC: 23 ms/s for 21,500 calls; here maybe 1-2 us each, ~7-15 ms/s). The back
  idles 345-360 ms/s in T84 walks, but under the full frame wait it is the frame's long pole.
- Memory: per 3D channel that runs back macros, one more MacroEngine (~70 KB) with its own Xbyak
  JIT program (1 MiB AutoGrow buffer), recompiled after each macro code change; FrontState ~57 KB.
- A macro code change after back macros were sent waits for them ("back code") and the next calls
  are analysed again (one AnalyzeEffects per distinct macro); a game uploading macro code every
  frame would pay that every frame (OoT: read "back code" in the stats).
- Logs: the back's Execute logs its first 400 DMA reads ("Native MME DMA read ...") once and its
  0x556 histogram ("Native MME render enable modes") every 20,000 writes (~every 3 s in the walk),
  as without the split.
- Not checked together: EDEN_UP_FAST_DRAW (every back macro call ends draw reuse: a Call entry
  without FastDrawNeutral), EDEN_UP_QUERY_LIGHT/QUERY_STATS (their front notes miss back reads),
  EDEN_NX2_MME_NATIVE (no native macro reads memory, so none of them runs on the back),
  EDEN_UP_OOT_FRONT_WAIT (parked).
- LIST_FETCH (both rules): a list on fresh pages written by a writer sent after the last host sync
  (or, for #1056, by a draw) is read as the CPU submitted it, as hardware fetch ordering allows; a
  stale list can lose the device or hang. The back-side check logs any such entry. #1056's rule
  differs from LISTSYNC only after a writer sent since the last sync.
- L1055_LIST_FETCH waits more than LISTSYNC, and more than T84 when the last sync followed the last
  writer.

Expected (Ocarina, docked full res, full frame wait; basis: EDEN_NX2_SPLIT_WAITS in the gameplay
windows (>= 1.5M ring words/s) of the T84 controls run638/run642 (t84-b) and T83 controls
run610/run614 (t83-r): front waited 419-438 ms/s, back idle 344-362 ms/s; list writers 157-167
ms/s at 3,400-3,700 waits/s; dma snapshot miss 149-163 ms/s at 1,370-1,470 drains/s (windows
66-235); acquire check 87-91 ms/s; acquire poll 17-24 ms/s; the macro DMA read wait is as large as
the list-writer wait, not smaller):
- BACK_MACROS: removes the "dma snapshot miss" site (~155 ms/s, 66-235) and the front's own work for
  ~7,000 macro calls/s (JIT run, snapshot lookups); adds ~7-15 ms/s of back work. Removed waits
  move: LISTSYNC cut list writers 160 -> 113 ms/s and acquire check rose 90 -> 131, total front
  waiting unchanged (428 vs 419-438), yet +0.6 fps; #949's acquire part cut acquire check 90 -> 46,
  total -16 ms/s, yet -0.7 fps (t84-b). So: net front wait -0 to -80 ms/s; fps 0 to +1.0 (0 to +7%), best
  guess +0.5 (+3-4%) alone, +0.8 to +1.2 with LISTSYNC or L1056_LIST_FETCH (different sync families,
  partly additive). Upstream: OoT Kokiri +8% (34.8 -> 37.6 fps, 5900X + RTX 3080, both parts);
  #1057's tester +12% from the back macros (32.1 -> 36.0).
- L1056_LIST_FETCH: LISTSYNC's 285 waits/s and 113 ms/s (t83-r run611) minus the waits where a
  writer was sent after the last sync; expect 0-30 ms/s less than LISTSYNC, fps = LISTSYNC's +0.6
  over the control within +-0.2. The stats line in a LISTSYNC arm gives the exact share (LISTSYNC's
  vs #1056's verdicts).
- L1055_LIST_FETCH: waits >= LISTSYNC's; expect 0 to +0.4 over the control, possibly negative. No
  slot unless L1056_LIST_FETCH logs a stale entry (then it is the stricter fallback); else retire.

Test arm (T84 + this diff; play env v2.1 docked with the full frame wait, the T84 batch env, walk
script; every arm EDEN_UP_L1056_STATS=1 and EDEN_NX2_SPLIT_WAITS=1; at most 5 tests a batch, each
batch with its own controls, ~3 valid runs per arm):
- c (control): nothing else.
- m: EDEN_UP_L1056_BACK_MACROS=1.
- ml: EDEN_UP_L1056_BACK_MACROS=1 EDEN_UP_L1056_LIST_FETCH=1 (#1056 as merged).
- s: EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_LISTSYNC=1 (the current list candidate).
- v (1 run, correctness, not for fps): ml + EDEN_NX2_SPLIT_VERIFY=1.
Batch 1: c, m, c, m, v. Stop there if v logs a "Split front register" or "Split front shadow
register" difference, an escape or a "split back macro ... wrote outside" line, or an m run hangs
or crashes. Batch 2: m, c, ml, c, ml (m: 3 runs against 4 controls, every batch with its own).
Batch 3, only if m held up: s, ml, s, ml, s (ml against the current list candidate). Give
EDEN_UP_L1056_LIST_FETCH=1 alone (l) or with LISTSYNC (ms) a slot only if the controls' #1056 line
shows #1056's would-wait count clearly below LISTSYNC's (it can differ only after a writer sent
since the last sync; within ~5% l is s). EDEN_UP_L1055_LIST_FETCH gets no slot.
Proof the switch acted (m, ml, v): the start line "Lichii #1056: EDEN_UP_L1056_BACK_MACROS=1 ..."
(ml: list fetch rule #1056); "EDEN_UP_L1056_BACK_MACROS: split macro at 0x142 reads memory and
